#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1facf79-5511-59b8-91dc-8b52e5da3d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\ln\\frac{557}{93}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1facf79-5511-59b8-91dc-8b52e5da3d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\ln\\frac{557}{93}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406367} :answer-field/id #uuid "84976508-d8ea-53d9-b93e-9f9ee254578b" :answer-field/key "integral" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406253}] :question/id #uuid "740fb150-6096-5941-a88d-25c2d5508769" :question/problem "Source question: 4.\n\nUse the **Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals** to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r.\n$$ The field $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{5x}{5x^2+3y^2},\\frac{3y}{5x^2+3y^2}\\right\\rangle\n$$ is conservative with the supplied potential $f=\\ln\\sqrt{5x^2+3y^2}$. The piecewise smooth curve joins $(3,4)$ to $(5,12)$.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}." :question/worked-solution "$5(5)^2+3(12)^2=557$ and $5(3)^2+3(4)^2=93$. Thus $f(B)-f(A)=\\ln\\sqrt{557}-\\ln\\sqrt{93}$. The curve is understood to lie in the field domain, excluding the origin."}]}
